A lengthy index finger means a lower risk of developing cancer
Men whose index finger is a longer than the ring finger are obviously exposed to a lesser exercise to develop prostate cancer.
This is the surprising consideration of a study published on Wednesday by the British Journal of Cancer.
This should be related to the mirror of the masculine hormone testosterone.
"Our findings show that the relative length of his finger can serve as a single test for the risk of prostate cancer, especially among men up to sixty," said Ros Eeles co-written a chemist, Ros Eeles, professor at the cancer research institute at the university in Warwick.
In this way the finger could help to determine which men were supposed to undergo prostate cancer, especially when genetic risks or other risk factors occur, such as family health exertion, Ms Eeles added.
Between 1994-2009 she pursued more than 1,500 patients with prostate chemotherapy in nannies with her colleagues in Britain, but at the same same time 3000 healthy men.
With more than half the participants in the study the index finger of the right hand was shorter than the ring finger.
These men were considered the same probability of prostate chemotherapy as for 19 percent of those men whose ring and index finger were the same length.
With men whose index finger longer than the ring finger, however this risk decreased by 33 per cent, in men up to sixty even by 87 per cent.
Obviously reflects the different mirrors of sex hormones to which the child was exposed in the uterus.
Less testosterone matches a prolonged index finger.
Previous augments yielded that testosterone accelerates prostate cancer growth.
Václav Pases, a leading Czech geneticist and former chairwoman of the academy of many other sciences, said this study, althrtheless he does not consider the relationship between the finger length and cancer threat "comprehensibly proven".
